 "notes": ["Cash flow from the company's SEC filings (XBRL: operating cash flow NetCashProvidedByUsedInOperatingActivities, capex PaymentsToAcquirePropertyPlantAndEquipment, finance-lease principal FinanceLeasePrincipalPayments), drawn as the Capex tab's SEC cash-flow table: 10-Q cash-flow figures are year-to-date, so a quarter without its own 3-month figure is the difference of two (Q2 = H1 - Q1, Q4 = full year - 9 months). FCF = operating cash flow - capex; FCF after leases also subtracts the finance-lease principal payments. FCF QoQ / YoY only between two positive values.", "Q4 26: diluted EPS from Yahoo's reported EPS where it matches net income / diluted shares, else net income / diluted shares (Yahoo's statements lack it).", "Fiscal labels are the company's own fiscal year, named by the calendar year it ends in.", "PER uses the trailing 4 quarters' diluted EPS (annual: the year's), ROA the trailing 4 quarters' net income over average total assets. EPS (GAAP, diluted) is the reported figure; EPS (adjusted) is Yahoo Finance's EPS on the analysts' basis (for US companies usually the company's adjusted, non-GAAP EPS; elsewhere usually the reported one), the basis of the (E) consensus. Each has its own QoQ / YoY, so a reported figure is never compared with an adjusted one.", "Yahoo lists the adjusted EPS of the last 4 quarters only: each daily run records what it shows (market/history/financials_eps_adjusted.jsonl), so older quarters stay. A fiscal year's adjusted EPS is Yahoo's annual figure, else the sum of its four quarters when all four are known (listed in \"derived\"), else blank.", "Adjusted EPS of the quarters before the daily records is the \"Reported EPS\" of Yahoo's earnings calendar, the same basis (checked against Yahoo's current figures on the quarters both list) and already on today's share basis; each quarter is as first reported, so a later change of the company's adjusted-EPS definition is not restated."]
